---
title: Create a GitHub account
description: 'A GitHub signup guide, with the decisions worth making first: public name, private email and two-factor authentication.'
sidebar:
  order: 3
---

Your course lives and gets reviewed on GitHub, so you need an account to [connect as attribution](/en/profesores/github/). If you already have one, skip this page.

{% aside title="Decide the name before signing up" %}
Your GitHub username is **public**, and it's what will appear as the course author. If you'd rather use a pseudonym, create it now: changing it later breaks the links to your profile and your contributions.
{% /aside %}

## Signing up

{% steps %}

1. Open [github.com/signup](https://github.com/signup).

2. Enter your **email**. It can be different from the one you use on the campus.

3. Pick a **password**. GitHub requires at least 15 characters, or 8 with a number and a lowercase letter. Use a password manager.

4. Pick your **username**. This is the public part: `github.com/your-username`.

5. Solve the small anti-robot puzzle.

6. Enter the **verification code** sent to your email. If it doesn't show up, check spam.

7. GitHub asks some personalisation questions. **You can skip all of them.**

8. When it asks about a plan, choose **Free**. You don't need anything paid to publish a course.

{% /steps %}

## Turn on two-factor authentication

GitHub **requires it** for every account that contributes code, and submitting a course is contributing code. If you don't turn it on yourself, GitHub will eventually demand it and block you halfway through the submission.

Go to **Settings → Password and authentication → Two-factor authentication** and enable one of:

- **Authenticator app** (Aegis, Ente Auth, 2FAS, Bitwarden…) — the recommended option.
- **Security key** or passkey.
- **SMS** — only if you have no other option; it's the least secure.

{% aside type="danger" title="Save the recovery codes" %}
GitHub gives you a list of single-use codes when you enable 2FA. Keep them off your phone (printed, or in your password manager). Without them, losing your phone means losing the account — and with it, authorship of your course.
{% /aside %}

## Using a pseudonym? Hide your email

By default, the commits you make can expose your email address. Under **Settings → Emails**, tick:

- **Keep my email addresses private** — GitHub gives you a `…@users.noreply.github.com` address for commits.
- **Block command line pushes that expose my email** — stops it slipping out by accident from the terminal.

## What about signing in to the campus?

This same account works to [sign in to the campus with GitHub](/en/acceso/login-github-google/). One difference to watch: for that, the campus needs **a public or verified email** on your GitHub profile. If you've enabled email privacy and still want to sign in to the campus with GitHub, use [username and password](/en/acceso/crear-cuenta/) or [Google](/en/acceso/login-github-google/) for the campus, and keep GitHub for course authorship.

## Once you have it

Go back to your instructor space and [connect it in the attribution block](/en/profesores/github/).
